  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nQuiet, pleasant weather will persist into this weekend as high \npressure shifts off to the east and the mid level flow becomes \nincreasingly zonal with little in the way of low level moisture \nadvection or forcing. Low level flow will turn more to the south-\nsouthwest in this regime over the weekend allowing for a gradual \nwarming trend. Several weak perturbations in the height field \nlikely track east into the Great Lakes, one late tonight, and \nthen another Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. Convection \non the nose of a LLJ and developing MUCAPE axis likely remains \nto the northwest of the area with the main impact locally being \nan increase in mid-high level clouds with each passing wave. \nWith that said, cannot rule out decaying elevated showers \nsurviving into northern zones at some point Saturday night or \nSunday morning. \n\nBuilding heat and humidity then becomes the story next week, at \nleast Monday through Wednesday, as a ~595 dm upper ridge at 500 mb \nflexes north into the Ohio Valley and southern Great Lakes. Current \nprojections are for afternoon heat indices to reach well into the \n90s each day, especially across the southern half of the forecast \narea where ridge topping convective cloud debris is less likely to \nspill over. Warm mid level profiles should put a lid on any surface \nbased convection for the bulk of these periods, though cannot rule \nout convective outflow or elevated convection north of the US 30 \ncorridor, especially toward the middle of the week. Models then\ndiverge for the second half of the week on whether the ridging \nbreaks down enough to send a frontal boundary either into or \nthrough the area with low chances (20-30%) for showers/storms \nand slightly cooler temps.",
